      "The NDIS entrapment dimension of this case reveals a specific feature of Australian institutional architecture that the archive exposes with unusual precision. The National Disability Insurance Scheme was designed as a support mechanism for people with serious disability. The documentary record in this archive shows how that mechanism can be weaponised: by making a disabled person's economic survival dependent on NDIS support, and then using the administrative framework of the NDIS — assessments, reviews, compliance requirements, and the threat of support withdrawal — as a mechanism of control and coercion. The NDIS entrapment identified in this case is not necessarily unique to this case. It may represent a feature of the NDIS administrative framework that is available for use against any disabled person who has become inconvenient to government agencies — and who is economically dependent on a scheme those agencies administer.",

